# conversations-thanks.kb - Conversations / Thanks
# Format: concept | intent | answer | keywords | patterns | tl | bis
# 6 entries, one genuinely distinct fact per line.

thank you | definition | Thank you is the default English phrase for gratitude, and its power is precisely that it is unmarked, since anything more emphatic signals that the thanks are unusual. This is why people over-thank in small things and under-thank in large ones. The phrase becomes cold when it is used for something that deserved a real acknowledgement, and it becomes warm when it is used for something small, because the contrast is what carries the tone. | thank you, gratitude, warm, cold, default, contrast | how to say thank you, is thank you too formal, when to say thank you | thank you | salamat
you are welcome | definition | You are welcome is the standard reply to thanks, and its real function is to decline the debt rather than to accept the praise, which is why a small no problem works equally well. English is unusually rich here, offering no problem, my pleasure, any time and not at all, and they differ in warmth more than in meaning. The reply that fails is an over-emphatic one, since a long response to a small thanks implies the thanks were hard to hear. | welcome, reply, thanks, debt, praise, no problem | how do you reply to thank you, you are welcome vs no problem, is my pleasure formal | you are welcome | walay problema
appreciate | definition | To appreciate something is to value it, and it also means to be fully aware of something, as in I appreciate that you came. The two senses drift toward each other until the second becomes a polite formula meaning thank you, and that formula is where the interesting part is. Appreciate is used where thanks would sound too transactional, and it works best when followed by naming the specific thing rather than the gesture. | appreciate, value, aware, formula, specific, gesture | meaning of appreciate, is appreciate better than thank you, I appreciate that you came | appreciate | pahalaga
credit | definition | To give credit is to name the person who did the work, and the word carries a strong sense of it having been unclaimed. This is why credit is a social act rather than a factual one, since the fact of who did something is often already known. The passive matters too, because doing the credits or taking the credit describe opposite behaviours, and the difference shows up in who ends up being known for the thing. | credit, name, work, unclaimed, known, social | what does give credit mean, difference between giving and taking credit, why credit matters | credit | kredito
pay back | definition | To pay someone back is to return a favour or a sum of money, and the phrase covers both while keeping them distinct enough in practice. The figurative sense, as in pay back somebody who helped you years ago, is the one that gets used about relationships and grievances, and there it often carries a hint of retaliation rather than gratitude. That overlap is why pay back is one of the more ambiguous common phrases in English. | pay back, return, favour, money, revenge, gratitude | what does pay back mean, pay back a favour, difference between repay and pay back | pay back | babayaran
deserving | definition | Deserving means somebody earned what they got, and the word smuggles in a comparison because deserving is always relative to something. A person can deserve the promotion and not deserve the pay that came with it, since the two are assessed against different yardsticks. In ordinary conversation the word usually appears as denial, in you deserve better, and it works there because it validates the listener without adding any new information. | deserving, earn, promotion, yardstick, deny, validate | what does deserving mean, you deserve better meaning, is deserving negative | deserving | karapat-dapat
